WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.627
The final coding exercise combines WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, and AVG.

00:00:04.627 --> 00:00:06.212
Use only Fall twenty twenty-four rows.

00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:12.612
That semester condition applies to source rows, so think about WHERE.

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.287
Create one group per course.

00:00:14.287 --> 00:00:16.612
Calculate the average grade and enrollment.

00:00:16.612 --> 00:00:19.822
Then keep only courses with at least fifty students.

00:00:19.822 --> 00:00:25.637
Because enrollment is a grouped count, that threshold belongs after grouping.

00:00:25.637 --> 00:00:28.272
Sort with the highest average first.

00:00:28.272 --> 00:00:30.917
Run and Check when your query is ready.

00:00:30.917 --> 00:00:36.852
If a course with fewer than fifty students still appears, inspect the HAVING condition.

00:00:36.852 --> 00:00:41.102
If Spring rows affect the average, inspect the WHERE condition.

00:00:41.102 --> 00:00:46.712
Use the meaning of each clause to debug the result.

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:50.782
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:50.782 --> 00:00:55.827
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:55.827 --> 00:00:59.362
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:59.362 --> 00:01:03.717
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:10.797
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:10.797 --> 00:01:17.167
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:19.457
not only whether the statement executes.
